{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:17576282 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:17576282 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"17576282","source":"MED","pmid":"17576282","doi":"10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01916.x","title":"Putting feelings into words: affect labeling disrupts amygdala activity in response to affective stimuli.","authorString":"Lieberman MD, Eisenberger NI, Crockett MJ, Tom SM, Pfeifer JH, Way BM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lieberman MD","lastName":"Lieberman","firstName":"Matthew D","initials":"MD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095-1563, USA. lieber@ucla.edu"}]}},{"fullName":"Eisenberger NI","lastName":"Eisenberger","firstName":"Naomi I","initials":"NI"},{"fullName":"Crockett MJ","lastName":"Crockett","firstName":"Molly J","initials":"MJ"},{"fullName":"Tom SM","lastName":"Tom","firstName":"Sabrina M","initials":"SM"},{"fullName":"Pfeifer JH","lastName":"Pfeifer","firstName":"Jennifer H","initials":"JH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6771-1873"}},{"fullName":"Way BM","lastName":"Way","firstName":"Baldwin M","initials":"BM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9618-0979"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6771-1873"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9618-0979"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","recommended_reviews","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"18","journalIssueId":1402168,"dateOfPublication":"2007 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2007,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Psychol Sci","title":"Psychological science","essn":"1467-9280","issn":"0956-7976","nlmid":"9007542","isoabbreviation":"Psychol Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2007-05-01"},"pubYear":"2007","pageInfo":"421-428","abstractText":"Putting feelings into words (affect labeling) has long been thought to help manage negative emotional experiences; however, the mechanisms by which affect labeling produces this benefit remain largely unknown. Recent neuroimaging studies suggest a possible neurocognitive pathway for this process, but methodological limitations of previous studies have prevented strong inferences from being drawn. A functional magnetic resonance imaging study of affect labeling was conducted to remedy these limitations. The results indicated that affect labeling, relative to other forms of encoding, diminished the response of the amygdala and other limbic regions to negative emotional images. Additionally, affect labeling produced increased activity in a single brain region, right ventrolateral prefrontal cortex (RVLPFC). Finally, RVLPFC and amygdala activity during affect labeling were inversely correlated, a relationship that was mediated by activity in medial prefrontal cortex (MPFC). These results suggest that affect labeling may diminish emotional reactivity along a pathway from RVLPFC to MPFC to the amygdala.","affiliation":"University of California, Los Angeles, CA 90095-1563, USA. lieber@ucla.edu","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Amygdala","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AH","qualifierName":"anatomy & histology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prefrontal Cortex","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AH","qualifierName":"anatomy & histology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Facial Expression"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Brain Mapping","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Analysis of Variance"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Photic Stimulation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Emotions","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Affect","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cues"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Visual Perception","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Task Performance and Analysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sex Distribution"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2007.01916.x"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":582,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2007-08-10","dateOfCreation":"2007-06-20","dateOfRevision":"2011-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2007-05-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEva